    "created_at": "2022-05-09T09:13:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-05-09T09:13:57Z",
    "body": "I wrote the article that the OP is linking to. Verifying repository ownership (per CVE-2022-24765) doesn't defend against viable attack scenarios, such as having a victim user download and unpack a tarball (which will cause files to be owned by them) and then `cd`'ing into the directory. Or having the user `git clone` a repository (again causing files to be owned by them) containing an embedded bare repo, and then `cd`'ing into the directory containing the bare repo (unpatched by Git, although [discussions](https://public-inbox.org/git/kl6lsfqpygsj.fsf@chooglen-macbookpro.roam.corp.google.com/) on their public mailing list are ongoing as to what can be done)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
